[Unit]
Description=Clawcius ops executor — runs a host agent for tasks the sandboxed agents file
Documentation=file:/home/npurcell/clawcius/ops/README.md
After=network-online.target docker.service
Wants=network-online.target
Requires=docker.service

# NOT ordered Before= or After= the wakers, and NOT bound to them.
#
# Restarting clawcius.service is one of the operations this unit performs. A
# dependency in either direction would mean the executor is stopped or
# restarted as a side effect of the thing it is supposed to be restarting —
# which is the exact failure the separate daemon exists to avoid. It has to
# outlive the units it acts on.

# ── A BOUNDED start limit. This said "never give up" until 2026-08-19. ────
#
# [Unit] directives, not [Service], and that part is unchanged and still
# hard-won: in [Service] systemd IGNORES them with a warning and silently
# leaves the default of 5 starts per 10s. If you move these two lines you have
# not tightened the limit, you have replaced it with a much tighter one you did
# not choose.
#
# What changed is the values. They were both 0 — either one being 0 disables
# rate limiting entirely — and the reason given was: "This one holds rollback
# deadlines; it staying dead is how a broken rebuild becomes a permanent
# outage."
#
# THAT JUSTIFICATION EXPIRED ON 2026-08-16 AND NOBODY CAME BACK FOR THIS LINE.
# The check-in deadlines, the automatic rollback and the wake that drove them
# all went with the ops spool. Nothing arms a deadline any more — the only
# caller of `StateStore.arm()` left in the tree is the self-test — and
# `reportRetiredDeadlines()` runs at boot for the express purpose of CLEARING
# any deadline an older build left behind. This unit no longer holds anything
# that expires while it is down. It is a mailbox and a unit desk, and both of
# those are simply unavailable whether it is looping or dead.
#
# ── AND UNLIMITED RETRIES WERE NEVER BUYING AVAILABILITY ──────────────────
#
# This is the part worth keeping. A unit in a five-second restart loop is not
# more available than a unit in `failed` — it is doing no work in either state.
# What the loop buys is the APPEARANCE of availability: `Restart=always` with
# no limit means the unit never enters `failed`, so it never appears in
# `systemctl --failed`, which is the channel this project already relies on to
# send someone to read a failure (see ops/src/verify.ts). A permanent config
# error becomes indistinguishable from a healthy service to everything that
# watches.
#
# That is not hypothetical. Clawcius #89 is this unit failing to start 22,675
# consecutive times over 31 hours, inside the config loader, with the host
# agent dead the whole while and nothing escalating. #89 recommended a start
# limit and nobody did it.
#
# ── The numbers, and the arithmetic behind them ───────────────────────────
#
# RestartSec=5 and a config refusal dies in well under a second, so a genuine
# permanent failure cycles about every 5-6 seconds: 20 starts is roughly TWO
# MINUTES before the unit lands in `failed` where it can be seen. A transient
# that clears within those 20 attempts is ridden out exactly as before.
#
# Widen the burst if a real transient on this host is ever observed to need
# more than two minutes; do not set either value back to 0. Losing four extra
# minutes of automatic retry is worth 31 hours of visibility, and the trade only
# looks close if you believe the loop was accomplishing something.
#
# THE ONE SELF-CLEARING TRANSIENT IN THIS START PATH, named so the burst has
# something concrete to be sized against. Only two things throw at module scope
# in ops/src/index.ts: the config load, which is permanent by nature, and
# `takeLock`, which throws `another clawcius-ops is running as pid N` when the
# lock file names a live process. `shutdown()` releases the lock synchronously
# before exit, so that is NOT the supervised case — it is somebody on the box at
# 2am running `npm start` by hand next to the running service. It clears itself
# the moment their process exits, and it is also the case least likely to finish
# inside two minutes: debugging by hand does not. Before this limit existed the
# service came back on its own when they were done; now it is in `failed` until
# `reset-failed`. That is the intended trade — a visible `failed` is the point —
# and the operator who trips it is by definition already at the keyboard.
#
# RECOVERY, because a bounded limit needs one: a unit that has hit the limit
# stays dead until it is reset, and `systemctl restart` alone will NOT start it.
#
#   systemctl status clawcius-ops        # the refusal is in the journal above
#   systemctl reset-failed clawcius-ops
#   systemctl start clawcius-ops
#
# Fix the cause first. A reset with the bad config still in place buys another
# two minutes of looping and the same `failed`.
StartLimitIntervalSec=600
StartLimitBurst=20

[Service]
Type=simple

# Root, and this is the whole point of the design rather than laziness.
#
# The executor runs docker, and it starts the host agent session. The agents
# cannot: they are in gVisor containers with no docker socket and no host
# socket, deliberately, and docker/run-container.sh carries the note about why
# mounting one would make gVisor decorative.
#
# ── THIS UNIT IS NO LONGER A NARROW HOLE, AND SAYING SO IS THE POINT ──────
#
# It used to say "narrow because ops-config.yaml lists every unit, repo and
# instance it may touch by exact name". Since 2026-08-10 that is false. The verb
# list was replaced by a `task` verb carrying free text, handed to a headless
# Claude Code session running on this host with Bash and with passwordless sudo
# (ops/clawcius-sudoers). What bounds it is a snapshot before every task, an
# automatic rollback after a failed one, and a complete audit of every command
# in /var/lib/clawcius-ops/journal.jsonl.
#
# The operator accepted that trade explicitly, twice, in writing. Anybody
# hardening this unit needs to understand it before deciding what to add: the
# threat is no longer "a malformed request reaches systemctl", it is "a session
# with a shell does something nobody expected", and no directive in this file
# addresses that.
#
# ── THE SESSION IS NOT THIS UNIT'S USER, AND SINCE 2026-08-11 IT IS NOT ───
# ── THE OPERATOR EITHER ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# This unit is root because it holds the docker socket and starts the session.
# The SESSION is dropped, in the forked child before execve, to the
# unprivileged system account named by hostAgent.user in ops-config.yaml —
# `clawcius-ops` by default.
#
# Until 2026-08-11 it was dropped instead to "whoever owns the checkout", which
# on this host is npurcell, who is in the `docker` group, which SETUP.md itself
# calls "effectively root on the host". So the session was root-equivalent by a
# route with no sudo, no sudoers rule and no audit entry in it, and every
# hardening decision in this file was being made about the wrong process.
#
# ops/src/agent-user.ts now REFUSES to start a session as an account that does
# not exist, has uid 0, is in any root-equivalent group, or can read any
# configured secret — re-checked against the live /etc/group before every task.
# If you are reading this because tasks are being refused, read the boot banner
# in `journalctl -u clawcius-ops`: it names the account, the group and the
# `gpasswd -d` to run.
#
# What this unit still does NOT have, and must not gain: DISCORD_TOKEN,
# GITHUB_TOKEN, any Anthropic API key in an EnvironmentFile. The host agent
# authenticates through the SERVICE ACCOUNT's own Claude Code OAuth credentials
# under its own home (/var/lib/clawcius-ops), which is a second login that has
# to be established once by hand — see MIGRATION.md § 4 — and NOT through the
# operator's. ops/src/host-agent.ts REFUSES TO SPAWN if the environment it
# built contains anything that looks like a credential. Adding an
# EnvironmentFile here would be handing a shell session the ability to speak as
# the bot, which is the one thing this daemon's whole reporting design exists to
# prevent: it says nothing to anybody, it answers the coordinator that asked by
# DM, and the sandboxed agent — which holds the token — does the talking. (That
# answer was a file written into the requester's wake spool until 2026-08-16.
# The route changed; the reason this unit holds no token did not.)
User=root
Group=root

WorkingDirectory=/home/npurcell/clawcius/ops

Environment=PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
Environment=OPS_CONFIG_PATH=/home/npurcell/clawcius/ops/ops-config.yaml

# ── OPS_DRY_RUN IS DELIBERATELY NOT SET HERE ──────────────────────────────
#
# `OPS_DRY_RUN=false` is what makes the executor act: a Claude Code session
# with a shell and passwordless sudo runs on this host when a coordinator DMs
# the host agent. `true` means it gets no Bash tool at all and can only
# describe what it would have done. Read ops/README.md § The trust model before
# turning it on.
#
# THIS FILE DOES NOT SET IT, AND THAT ABSENCE IS THE DESIGN. Installing this
# unit is the first thing SETUP.md § Install tells an operator to do, and it
# does it with `enable --now`, fifteen lines before it tells them to confirm the
# boot line says DRY RUN. A committed `Environment=OPS_DRY_RUN=false` would make
# that step produce a LIVE daemon at the point the guide promises a dry-run one.
# Unset, this loader falls back to ops-config.yaml, which ships `true`: a fresh
# checkout installs safe by construction rather than by remembering.
#
# ── WHERE THIS HOST'S `false` LIVES: A DROP-IN, ALSO IN THIS REPOSITORY ────
#
#   systemd/clawcius-ops.service.d/live.conf
#     → /etc/systemd/system/clawcius-ops.service.d/live.conf
#
# That is the whole per-machine deviation, and it is one file the operator
# copies in SETUP.md § Going live. Everything the committed line bought is kept:
# it is tracked, so the deployed value has an owner and a record; the merged
# value still answers `systemctl show clawcius-ops -p Environment`; and
# `systemctl cat clawcius-ops` prints the drop-in below this unit, so a reader
# on the host sees it without a checkout.
#
# What it also buys is the direction of the failure. Forget the drop-in and the
# daemon boots DRY RUN and says so in its first line. Forget it under a
# committed `false` and the daemon boots LIVE and says so in a line nobody was
# told to read yet. Only one of those two mistakes is safe, and it is now the
# one that is easy to make.
#
# ── WHY NOT JUST EDIT ops-config.yaml ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# Because that is what was being done, and it broke a deploy. Until 2026-08-19
# the deployment expressed this by hand-editing line 55 of that tracked file and
# never committing the edit. That arrangement had no owner and no record: the
# difference existed only as a diff nobody looked at, it aborted
# `git merge --ff-only` the first time anything upstream touched the file —
# blocking a deploy of eight merged pull requests — and resolving that conflict
# the obvious way would have reverted the executor to dry run silently, leaving
# it accepting tasks and reporting success while doing nothing.
#
# The value must be exactly `true` or `false`. The loader refuses anything else
# and names the variable — it does NOT fall back, because `flase` or `0` quietly
# resolving to "act" is the failure this whole arrangement exists to prevent,
# and `0` is a truthy string in JavaScript.
#
# The environment WINS over the file. That precedence is stated once, in
# `DryRunSource` in ops/src/config.ts, and is not restated here on purpose.
#
# INSTALLING ANY OF THIS IS AN OPERATOR STEP. Committing a unit or a drop-in
# changes nothing on the host until someone copies it and runs
# `systemctl daemon-reload && systemctl restart clawcius-ops` — see SETUP.md.
# Until then the running unit keeps whatever environment it was started with.

ExecStart=/home/npurcell/.local/share/node/bin/node dist/index.js

Restart=always
RestartSec=5

# ── State ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# clawcius-ops holds the journal, the circuit breaker and any armed rollback
# deadline. 0750 and root-owned: no part of it is bind-mounted into any
# container. It is also where the audit log lives, which since 2026-08-10 is the
# primary record of what this machine has been told to do.
#
# clawcius-host-agent is the session's working directory and is a SIBLING, not a
# child, on purpose. The session runs as the unprivileged service account and
# needs to write its cwd; putting it inside a 0750 root-owned directory would
# mean either the session cannot traverse to it or clawcius-ops stops being
# 0750. systemd creates it root-owned and the executor chowns it — since
# 2026-08-11 to hostAgent.user rather than to the checkout's owner, which is now
# a different account. If that chown fails the boot log says so with the exact
# command; the symptom otherwise is `claude` failing to start with an ENOENT on
# the BINARY, which reads as "claude is not installed".
#
# Note the name collision, because it will confuse somebody: the service
# account's HOME is /var/lib/clawcius-ops and this unit's StateDirectory is
# also called clawcius-ops (/var/lib/clawcius-ops). They are THE SAME PATH, and
# that is wrong — the state directory holds the journal, the breaker and the
# armed deadlines and is 0750 root-owned, and the agent account must not own or
# write it. MIGRATION.md § 1 therefore creates the account with
# `--home-dir /var/lib/clawcius-agent`, NOT /var/lib/clawcius-ops. Do not let
# `useradd --system --create-home clawcius-ops` pick the default home; on
# Debian that is /home/clawcius-ops, which is harmless, but a hand-written
# `--home-dir /var/lib/clawcius-ops` would hand the breaker to the account it
# is meant to bound.
#
# It is deliberately NOT the checkout. Claude Code auto-discovers CLAUDE.md and
# project settings from its working directory, and the checkout is a tree the
# agents can get commits merged into; the config loader refuses a
# `hostAgent.workDir` inside any repo path, inside any directory bind-mounted
# into an instance's container — `<stateDir>/run`, `<stateDir>/workspaces` and
# `<stateDir>/agent-home`, for every instance — or inside or containing the
# executor's own stateDir. Until 2026-08-16 the mount half of that was written
# as "any spool"; the spools are gone and the check names the mounts.
StateDirectory=clawcius-ops clawcius-host-agent
StateDirectoryMode=0750

# ── PATH: a real one, and it is load-bearing ──────────────────────────────
#
# Audited 2026-08-10. The Environment=PATH below is the executor's own, and it
# does NOT contain node — on this host node lives under /home/npurcell/.local —
# which is why ExecStart names the interpreter by absolute path. That is fine
# for this process and would be fatal for the host agent, whose tasks run npm
# and node by name.
#
# So the session does not inherit this PATH. ops/src/host-agent.ts builds its
# environment from scratch and constructs a PATH containing the claude
# directory, the npm directory, and the usual system paths including the sbin
# ones. Nothing here needs changing for that, and this note exists so that the
# next person to "tidy up" the PATH below knows which of the two they are
# looking at.

# ── No instance path is named here, and that was checked twice ────────────
#
# On 2026-08-10 the ops spool became one directory per instance
# (/var/lib/clawcius/run/ops and /var/lib/hamachi/run/ops rather than the
# single shared one), which is the kind of change that quietly needs a unit
# edit. This file was re-read line by line looking for anywhere the old path
# was named or implied. There was nowhere. On 2026-08-16 the spools were
# retired outright — no directory is a queue any more, a task arrives as a DM
# to `<crew>-host` on the Clawsky board — and the same re-read found the same
# answer, for the same reasons, which is why they are worth keeping written
# down rather than rediscovered a third time:
#
#   - no ReadWritePaths, so no list to extend;
#   - no ProtectSystem (=strict breaks the docker socket; see below), so the
#     filesystem is not read-only to begin with;
#   - no ProtectHome (it was removed after it made `pull` impossible, and would
#     now also stop the host agent finding its own credentials), so no second
#     exception to keep in step;
#   - StateDirectory= names clawcius-ops and clawcius-host-agent, which are the
#     executor's own state and the host agent's working directory. Neither is
#     inside any instance's state directory, and the loader refuses a config in
#     which either of them meets a container's bind mount.
#
# So this unit needs no change, and the reason is written down rather than
# assumed — two units in this repository shipped with hardening that made them
# unable to run at all (MemoryDenyWriteExecute in #7, ProtectHome here), both
# times because a directive was reasoned about and never loaded.
#
# The general point is the one that survived both dates: the paths that moved
# are owned by ops-config.yaml and agent-config.yaml, and a unit file that
# repeated them would have been a second copy to keep in step — the copy nobody
# edits, because it does not look like configuration. Do not add them here.
#
# IF ANYONE ADDS ReadWritePaths= LATER: it must list hostAgent.workDir, AND
# /home/npurcell, AND everywhere a task might need to write — which, since a
# task is free text, is "the filesystem". That is the honest reason there is no
# ReadWritePaths here and probably never will be: a path allowlist in front of a
# session with a shell is a list somebody maintains until the first task it
# breaks, and then widens to `/`.

# ── Re-audited 2026-08-11, against the new identity ───────────────────────
#
# The session stopped being npurcell and became an unprivileged service
# account. That changes what several directives here are protecting, so every
# one of them was re-read rather than assumed. The conclusions, in full:
#
#   User=root                 UNCHANGED. This unit holds the docker socket and
#                             performs the drop; it is the session that is not
#                             root, and it never was this unit.
#   NoNewPrivileges=false     UNCHANGED, and now more load-bearing: sudo is the
#                             session's ONLY privileged path, where before it
#                             also had the docker group.
#   ProtectHome (absent)      UNCHANGED VERDICT, DIFFERENT REASON. HOME moved
#                             out of /home; the checkout did not. See below.
#   RestrictSUIDSGID=true     KEPT, with a new caveat about setgid directories
#                             in the checkout. See below.
#   StateDirectory            UNCHANGED, but the chown target changed from the
#                             checkout's owner to hostAgent.user, and there is
#                             a name collision worth knowing about. See above.
#   SupplementaryGroups=      NOT ADDED, and it would do nothing here: it
#                             applies to the unit's own User=, which is root.
#                             The session's supplementary groups are set in
#                             ops/src/host-agent.ts around the spawn, because
#                             they have to be the AGENT's and systemd has no
#                             directive for "the groups of a process this unit
#                             forks with a different uid".
#   User= the agent instead   REJECTED. Running this whole unit as
#                             clawcius-ops would mean the executor could not
#                             use the docker socket (snapshots, rollbacks, the
#                             health sample) except by putting the agent
#                             account in the docker group — which is precisely
#                             what the whole 2026-08-11 rework exists to
#                             forbid. The split is the design: a root
#                             supervisor that is small and readable, and an
#                             unprivileged session that is neither.
#
# ── Hardening ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Deliberately light, and the reason is worth writing down rather than
# rediscovering. This unit exists to run systemctl and docker; the usual
# sandboxing directives are aimed at services that should not be able to do
# either. ProtectSystem=strict breaks the docker socket connection (writing to
# a unix socket inode needs write permission on it, which a read-only /run
# denies) — the same thing that constrains clawcius.service, for the same
# reason. PrivateDevices and ProtectKernelTunables interfere with docker's own
# operation.
#
# Anything added here must be verified with an actual restart and a real
# dry-run request, not by reading. A broken executor looks exactly like an
# agent whose requests are being ignored. That sentence was already in this
# file on 2026-08-09 and it was still not enough; see the ProtectHome block
# below for what shipped anyway.

# NOT MemoryDenyWriteExecute — audited 2026-08-10, after it turned out to be
# fatal in clawcius-status.service (#7). It was never in this unit, and it must
# never be added: V8 compiles JavaScript at runtime and needs pages mapped
# writable and then executable, which is precisely the transition the option
# forbids, so Node cannot start under it at all. This is not tunable and not
# specific to any one service — it is what a JIT is. The same audit is the
# reason for the rest of this block.

# NoNewPrivileges — false, and as of 2026-08-10 this is MANDATORY rather than
# merely cautious. Do not flip it. Re-checked 2026-08-11 against the new
# identity and the argument is unchanged in every particular.
#
# NNP forbids *gaining* privilege at execve, which makes the setuid bit a no-op.
# `sudo` is a setuid binary. The host agent session runs as the unprivileged
# service account (hostAgent.user) and does its privileged work through the
# rules in ops/clawcius-sudoers — so under NNP=true every one of those sudo
# calls fails, and fails with a message about being unable to set the effective
# uid that reads like a broken sudoers file rather than like a unit setting. The
# single most useful capability this service has would stop working, and the
# diagnosis would go looking in the wrong file.
#
# That got MORE important on 2026-08-11, not less. The old grantee reached
# docker through group membership and could have done much of this work without
# sudo at all; the new one cannot do any of it that way, by design, so sudo is
# now the only privileged path there is and NNP would remove all of it.
#
# Note the asymmetry, because it is the thing that used to be written here and
# is still true: DROPPING privilege is unaffected by NNP. The session is dropped
# to the service account by libuv calling setgid(2)/setuid(2) in the forked
# child from the uid/gid spawn options, before execve, with no setuid binary
# involved — and, since 2026-08-11, with the parent's supplementary groups set
# to the account's own for the duration of the spawn (ops/src/host-agent.ts,
# `withSupplementaryGroups`), because otherwise the child inherits root's group
# list and "this account is in no root-equivalent group" would be false of the
# running process. All of that half works fine under NNP. It is the sudo the
# session itself runs that would not.
#
# The old reasons also still hold: docker/run-container.sh runs `docker run`
# with --cap-drop sets and a gVisor runtime, and this unit's children include
# systemctl talking to the system bus, and neither has been tested under NNP
# here.
NoNewPrivileges=false

# Restricts *creating* files with the setuid/setgid bit, via seccomp on chmod
# and friends. It does not touch setuid(2) itself, so the session's privilege
# drop is unaffected, and it does not stop *executing* an existing setuid binary
# — checked deliberately on 2026-08-10, because if it did it would break sudo
# and therefore the host agent, in the same way NNP would. It does not.
#
# ── RE-CHECKED 2026-08-11, AND THIS ONE NEARLY BROKE ──────────────────────
#
# The shared-group scheme in MIGRATION.md § 2 puts the SETGID bit on every
# directory in the checkout, so that files the agent creates inherit the shared
# group and stay readable and writable by the operator. `chmod g+s` on a
# DIRECTORY is exactly the operation this directive's seccomp filter blocks.
#
# It does not break, for one reason: those chmods are run BY THE OPERATOR, from
# their own shell, during the migration — not by a task, and therefore not
# inside this unit's cgroup. If a future task ever needs to create a setgid
# directory (a new instance's state tree, say) it will fail here with an EPERM
# on chmod that looks nothing like a systemd setting, and the answer is that
# the operator runs that one command. That is an acceptable, and now written
# down, cost.
#
# What it otherwise costs: a task that legitimately needs to install a setuid
# binary fails here, loudly. That is the correct outcome for a root daemon's
# cgroup and it has never come up.
RestrictSUIDSGID=true

# A no-op on this unit, kept because it costs nothing and stops being a no-op
# the day someone changes User=. systemd documents RemoveIPC as having no
# effect on objects owned by root.
RemoveIPC=true

# ── NOT ProtectHome. Fatal before; still fatal, for a different reason. ──
#
# Re-audited 2026-08-11, after the session stopped running as npurcell.
#
# The 2026-08-10 case against it was that the session's HOME was /home/npurcell
# and that is where Claude Code keeps its OAuth credentials. THAT ARGUMENT NO
# LONGER APPLIES: HOME is now /var/lib/clawcius-agent, which ProtectHome does
# not touch. It would have been easy to conclude from that alone that
# ProtectHome=read-only is now safe to add. It is not, and the reason is the
# original one from 2026-08-09, which never went away:
#
#   THE CHECKOUT IS /home/npurcell/clawcius. `git pull` writes .git/, `npm ci`
#   writes node_modules/, `npm run build` writes dist/. Under
#   ProtectHome=read-only all three get EROFS on a filesystem that is
#   demonstrably read-write from a shell.
#
# And a near miss that also survives: ExecStart is
# /home/npurcell/.local/share/node/bin/node, so =yes or =tmpfs hides the
# interpreter and the unit does not start at all. Perversely the better failure
# — noticed on the first restart rather than the first build.
#
# So: still absent, and this block now records that its *justification* changed
# on 2026-08-11 while its *verdict* did not. That distinction is exactly the
# kind of thing that gets lost, and losing it is how a directive that is fatal
# for reason B gets added because reason A was fixed. The original argument
# follows.
#
#
# This unit carried ProtectHome=read-only. It reads like obvious hygiene: a
# root daemon has no business writing to anybody's home directory. On this host
# it makes two of the seven verbs impossible, and it does so silently enough
# that the cause is not obvious from the failure.
#
# The checkout is /home/npurcell/clawcius. `git pull` writes .git/; `npm ci`
# writes node_modules/; `npm run build` writes dist/. Under ProtectHome all
# three get EROFS on a filesystem that is demonstrably read-write when you look
# at it from a shell, so the journal fills with permission errors against paths
# whose `ls -l` says they are fine. The `pull` verb could never have worked as
# shipped, and it would have been diagnosed as a git problem.
#
# Note also the near miss: ExecStart is /home/npurcell/.local/share/node/bin/node.
# ProtectHome=yes or =tmpfs would have hidden the interpreter and the unit would
# not have started at all — which, perversely, would have been the better
# failure, because it would have been noticed on the first restart instead of
# on the first pull.
#
# The honest accounting is that ProtectHome bought approximately nothing here.
# This unit holds the docker socket. Anything it wanted to do to a home
# directory it can still do with `docker run -v /home:/host`, one command away,
# with no namespace in the path. Paying for a boundary that a process can step
# around in a single argument, with the two verbs that actually maintain the
# deployment, is not a trade worth making.
#
# ReadWritePaths=/home/npurcell/clawcius on top of ProtectHome=read-only is the
# tidier-looking fix and it is a real one. It is not what is here, because it
# has never been loaded on this host, and shipping reasoned-but-unloaded
# hardening into this exact file is what #7 was. If someone wants it, it goes
# in with a restart, a real `pull` in dry-run, and a real one out of it.

# ── Considered and deliberately absent ────────────────────────────────────
#
# SystemCallFilter — no. The children of this unit are docker, systemctl, git,
# npm and node, plus bash for run-container.sh and snapshot.sh. @system-service
# would cover the interesting ones (the build's setgid/setuid live in @setuid,
# which @system-service includes), but "would cover" is exactly the phrase that
# preceded the SIGTRAP restart loop in #7. A filter that is one syscall short
# on this unit does not degrade: it kills a redeploy halfway through, with the
# container already destroyed.
#
# IPAddressDeny — no, and this is not close. `git pull` reaches github.com and
# `npm ci` reaches the npm registry; IPAddressDeny=any would break both and
# would break them as timeouts rather than as refusals. clawcius-status.service
# can carry it because it genuinely talks to nothing; this unit is the one that
# fetches the code.
#
# PrivateTmp — no. npm stages package tarballs through TMPDIR, and while a
# private /tmp would technically serve, it also hides the unit's /tmp from
# every other process on the host, including the operator running `ls` during
# an incident. This unit is debugged by hand at 3am by definition.
#
# ProtectSystem — see the paragraph at the top: strict breaks the docker socket.
# =full is very likely fine and is likewise untested here.

# Bounded so a runaway operation cannot take the host with it.
#
# 512M/128 originally, raised to 2G/512 when the build became a step, and raised
# again to 6G/1024 on 2026-08-10 for the host agent. systemd's MemoryMax applies
# to the unit's WHOLE CGROUP, children included, and the children are now:
#
#   this daemon (node, small)
#   + a `claude` session (node, and not small — it holds a conversation)
#   + whatever that session runs, which is expected to include `npm ci` and
#     `tsc` over three packages, concurrently with the session still being alive
#
# 2G was sized for the second and third of those without the middle one. Under
# it the failure mode is the cgroup OOM-killing a build halfway through — a
# build failure with no compiler error in it, which is exactly the bad half-hour
# this file already has a paragraph about — or, worse, killing the session
# mid-task and leaving the host in whatever state it had reached.
#
# 6G is not a measurement. It is headroom on a box with enough RAM to spare it,
# chosen because the cost of it being too high is nothing until something runs
# away, and the cost of it being too low is a task that fails for a reason that
# appears nowhere in its own logs. If this host ever gets tight, measure with
# `systemd-cgtop` during a real task before lowering it.
#
# The containers a task starts do NOT count against these: docker places them
# in their own cgroup slice with their own --memory.
MemoryMax=6G
TasksMax=1024

StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal
SyslogIdentifier=clawcius-ops

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
